Dear everyone,

I'm having some issues with centering. I'm trying to center the data using indProd() for a second-order interaction and I noticed that the algorithm crashes if I choose anything except mean centering. More specifically, I get this error:

Error in matrix(rep(dat1[, i], length(var3)), ncol = length(var3)) * matrix(rep(dat2[,  :
  non-numeric argument to binary operator

I double-checked everything and all the introduced variables are numeric. Then I thought the problem was that one of the variables had only a single indicator, so I added some additional indicators, but I still got the same message. Then I changed the order of variables and, in brief, the error occurs whenever I introduce the third variable, regardles which variable it is. So, the algorithm seems to fail when the third variable is introduced. Has anyone else noticed this issue? Or, even better, is there a solution available? These seem to be the code lines of indProd where the error occurs (although this is just my assumption according to the error message):

for (i in 1:length(var1)) {
                for (j in 1:length(var2)) {
                  datProd3way <- data.frame(datProd3way, matrix(rep(dat1[,
                    i], length(var3)), ncol = length(var3)) *
                    matrix(rep(dat2[, j], length(var3)), ncol = length(var3)) *
                    dat3)
                }

Best regards,

From ub8fd92e5e3662745 at groups.google.com  Mon Nov  1 15:35:49 2021
From: ub8fd92e5e3662745 at groups.google.com (Stas Kolenikov)
Date: Mon, 01 Nov 2021 15:35:49 +0000
Subject: Re: se="bootstrap" changes test="Satorra.Bentler"
Message-ID: <fv8A8okRbgA.Jg-dyC-8FgAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <fv8A8okRbgA.9Gdl_fOxFgAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <fv8A8okRbgA.9Gdl_fOxFgAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/fv8A8okRbgA/m/Jg-dyC-8FgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The nature of the Satorra-Bentler correction is to adjust for the deviations of the variance-covariance matrix of the moment conditions/estimating equations from what would have been expected under the normal theory. I guess in this case, lavaan makes the call to use whatever non-MVNT matrix it has at hand... since the bootstrap produces a different covariance matrix than option "robust" does, the numbers are different.

The simulation work that I did when Ken Bollen and I worked on some Heywood papers convinced me that the "robust" option as is traditionally implemented in LISREL or MPlus underestimates the standard errors, as it fakes the "meat" of the sandwich estimate as a variance instead of MSE, and misses the misspecification biases. The true sandwich estimate (Stata's robust option) has its own underestimation problems, but at least they are supposed to go away asymptotically -- MPlus' "robust" option problems don't. (The propagation of inaccurate, overloading names for the methods is infuriating, but I was not the one inventing any of that.)

