Hi all,

Since the product indicators approach is currently the only
available option in Lavaan for testing moderations, I am wondering if you could give me a feedback on my issue.

My problem regards the controversy on item parcelling and the way in which I am specifying my parcels.

I have a second-order factor composed of 4 first-order factors. This
second-order factor is a moderator. I would like to proceed with a parcelling strategy to test the moderation using a product
indicators approach. However, I don’t want to parcel the first-orders using its
own indicators, because it would create problems when matching these parcels with
the indicators of the independent variable (due to the multidimensionality of
the moderator). Therefore, I was thinking to create parcels that contain one
item of each first-order factor - but I could not find any reference for that
so I am not sure if it would be acceptable.

 For example, based on this factors structure:

firstorder_1=~ item1 + item2 + item3 + item4
firstorder_2=~ item5 + item6 + item7 + item8
firstorder_3=~ item9 + item10 + item11 + item12
firstorder_4=~ item13 + item14 + item15 + item16

second=~ firstorder_1 + firstorder_2 + firstorder_3 + firstorder_4

I would create the parcels as follow:

df %<>%
rowwise %>%
mutate(parc1=mean(c(item1,
item5, item9, item13))) %>%
ungroup

 --then I repeat this procedure to create the other three
parcels.

 At the end of this procedure, I would create the product
indicators using the parcels:

df<-indProd(df, var1 = c (“x1 + x2” + “x3”, + “x4”),
var2 = c (“parc1”,
“parc2”, “parc3”, “parc4”),
doubleMC =
T, match=T,
namesProd
= c('int1', 'int2', 'int3', 'int4')
)

The parcels are only used for the interaction term; the structural model would still have the second-order factor regressing into the outcome:

mod<-"

firstorder_1=~ item1 + item2 + item3 + item4
firstorder_2=~ item5 + item6 + item7 + item8
firstorder_3=~ item9 + item10 + item11 + item12
firstorder_4=~ item13 + item14 + item15 + item16

second=~ firstorder_1 + firstorder_2 + firstorder_3 + firstorder_4

int=~ int1 + int2 + int3 + int4

X=~ x1 + x2 + x3+ x4
Y=~ y1 + y2 + y3 + y4 + y5

Y ~ second + int + X

"
 The significance of the interaction, along with fit
statistics (for both CFA and SEM), remains stable using either this parcelling
strategy or testing the interaction using only the first-orders separately. However,
the parcelling strategy would allow me to test one model only rather than four
different models for each first-order factor (I would not have enough power to
test 4 interactions in one model). The other options could be to pass to another
interaction strategy but I cannot use Mplus and I would prefer to stay within the
Lavaan/semTools environment (I would also like to avoid Bayesian method).

 In summary, my question is: would it be acceptable
to use this parcelling strategy? So that
each parcel contains “a bit” of each dimension?

Thank you very much!

